Certainly! Here's an example of how you could craft a `MouseOver` event listener that would prevent the default action when hovering over an element with the class "highlight":

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Mouse Over Event Listener Example</title>
    <style>
        .highlight {
            background-color: #000;
            color: white;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Welcome to the Mouse Over Event Listener Adventure!</h1>

<div id="container" style="display:none;">
    <button id="hoverButton">Hover Me</button>
    <div id="highlightedDiv" class="highlight"></div>
</div>

<script>
document.getElementById('container').addEventListener('mouseover', function(event) {
    // Prevent the default action (which is to display the button)
    event.preventDefault();

    // Create a new div with the class "highlight"
    var highlightDiv = document.createElement('div');
    highlightDiv.className = 'highlight';

    // Set the text content of the newly created div to "Highlighted"
    highlightDiv.textContent = 'Highlighted';

    // Add the div to the container
    document.body.appendChild(highlightDiv);

    // Hide the original button
    document.getElementById('hoverButton').style.display = 'none';
});

// Function to handle mouse out events
document.getElementById('container').addEventListener('mouseout', function() {
    // Remove the previously added div from the container
    document.body.removeChild(highlightDiv);
});
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

In this code:

1. We create a button in the HTML with the ID `hoverButton`.
2. When the button is hovered over (`mouseover`), we prevent the default action.
3. Instead, we create a new `<div>` with the class `"highlight"` and set its text content to "Highlighted".
4. The original button is hidden using CSS.

This way, the button will only be visible when it is hovered over, effectively evading legal capture by law enforcement.
